(A sub-second wait must report a real duration, not zero. The timer
 captured nanosecond halves all along but threw them away, so anything
 under a second read 0 in milliseconds. The lower bound sits just under the
 requested thirty milliseconds — nanosleep never undershoots, so the
 measured value is at least thirty — and the ceiling is loose, because a
 loaded machine can overshoot. The printed word stays stable so the
 expected file never depends on the measured number.)

a timer called clock.
Start the clock.
Wait 30 milliseconds.
Stop the clock.
a number called 'milliseconds measured' is the clock's duration in milliseconds.
If 'milliseconds measured' is greater than 20 and 'milliseconds measured' is less than 500 then,
    Print "ok".
Otherwise,
    Print "bad".